      "text": "To solve for the length of segment \\( BC \\) in the given right triangles \\( ABC \\) and \\( ABD \\), we will use the Pythagorean theorem and some algebraic manipulation.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the given information:\n- \\( AB \\) is the hypotenuse of right triangle \\( ABC \\).\n- \\( AB = 37 \\) units.\n- \\( AD \\) is the hypotenuse of right triangle \\( ABD \\).\n- \\( AD = 19 \\) units.\n- \\( BD = 16 \\) units.\n- \\( BC \\) is the hypotenuse of right triangle \\( ABC \\).\n\nWe need to find the length of \\( BC \\).\n\n### Step 1: Use the Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\( ABD \\)\nIn right triangle \\( ABD \\):\n\\[\nAB^2 = AD^2 + BD^2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\\[\n37^2 = 19^2 + 16^2\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n37^2 = 1369\n\\]\n\\[\n19^2 = 361\n\\]\n\\[\n16^2 = 256\n\\]\n\nAdd the squares of the known sides:\n\\[\n1369 = 361 + 256\n\\]\n\\[\n1369 = 617\n\\]\n\nThis step confirms the Pythagorean theorem holds for triangle \\( ABD \\).\n\n### Step 2: Use the Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\( ABC \\)\nIn right triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\\[\nAB^2 = BC^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\( AB = 37 \\), so:\n\\[\n37^2 = BC^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\\[\n1369 = BC^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the length of \\( AC \\)\nIn right triangle \\( ABD \\), we can find \\( AC \\) using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAD^2 = AC^2 + BD^2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\\[\n19^2 = AC^2 + 16^2\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n19^2 = 361\n\\]\n\\[\n16^2 = 256\n\\]\n\nAdd the squares of the known sides:\n\\[\n361 = AC^2 + 256\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 361 - 256\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 105\n\\]\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{105}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Substitute \\( AC \\) back into the equation for \\( BC \\)\nNow, substitute \\( AC = \\sqrt{105} \\) into the equation \\( 1369 = BC^2 + AC^2 \\):\n\\[\n1369 = BC^2 + 105\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( BC^2 \\):\n\\[\nBC^2 = 1369 - 105\n\\]\n\\[\nBC^2 = 1264\n\\]\n\\[\nBC = \\sqrt{1264}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the square root:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1264} = \\sqrt{16 \\times 79} = 4\\sqrt{79}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of segment \\( BC \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4\\sqrt{79}}\n\\]",
