    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of segment \\( BC \\) in the given right triangles \\( ABC \\) and \\( ABD \\), we will use the Pythagorean theorem. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nFirst, let's consider triangle \\( ABC \\). In triangle \\( ABC \\), \\( AB = 16 \\) units, \\( AC = 19 \\) units, and \\( BC \\) is the unknown hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAB^2 + AC^2 = BC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n16^2 + 19^2 = BC^2\n\\]\nCalculating the squares:\n\\[\n256 + 361 = BC^2\n\\]\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n617 = BC^2\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides to solve for \\( BC \\):\n\\[\nBC = \\sqrt{617}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's verify this result with triangle \\( ABD \\). In triangle \\( ABD \\), \\( AB = 16 \\) units, \\( AD = 37 \\) units, and \\( BD \\) is the unknown hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAB^2 + AD^2 = BD^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n16^2 + 37^2 = BD^2\n\\]\nCalculating the squares:\n\\[\n256 + 1369 = BD^2\n\\]\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n1625 = BD^2\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides to solve for \\( BD \\):\n\\[\nBD = \\sqrt{1625}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( BD \\) is the same as \\( AB \\) in triangle \\( ABC \\) (both are 16 units), we can confirm that the length of \\( BC \\) is indeed:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{617}}\n\\]",
